cs_content_header = cs_long_name
</python>

A course can attach roles and other metadata to individual users through its
`__USERS__` directory.  Each file is trusted Python named after the user's
authenticated username:

```text
numerical_methods/
├── preload.py
├── content.catsoop
└── __USERS__/
    ├── ada.py
    ├── grace.py
    └── course_queue.py
```

When `ada` visits the course, CAT-SOOP executes `__USERS__/ada.py` and merges
the variables it defines into `cs_user_info`.  The filename supplies the
username, so the file should not set `username` itself.

<tableofcontents/>

<catsoop-section>A Typical User File</catsoop-section>

A student file can be as small as:

```python
# __USERS__/ada.py
role = "Student"
full_name = "Ada Lovelace"
email = "ada@example.edu"
section = "L01"
```

A staff file often needs only a different role:

```python
# __USERS__/grace.py
role = "TA"
full_name = "Grace Hopper"
email = "grace@example.edu"
```

The `role` values must match keys in the course's `cs_permissions` mapping.
See [Creating New Pages](COURSE/docs/authoring/pages) for a role and permission
example.

<catsoop-section>Standard and Common Fields</catsoop-section>

Because a user file is Python, it does not have a rigid schema.  CAT-SOOP
directly interprets a small set of fields, while courses and plugins may define
additional ones.

* **`role`** selects an entry from `cs_permissions`.  If the user has neither
  an explicitly assigned role nor one supplied by authentication, CAT-SOOP
  uses `cs_default_role`.
* **`permissions`** directly supplies this user's permissions instead of
  deriving them from `role`.  This is useful for an exceptional account, but a
  role is usually easier to audit and maintain.
* **`name`** is the user's display name used by CAT-SOOP authentication and
  account interfaces.  An authentication provider may already supply it.
* **`email`** is the user's email address.  CAT-SOOP's mail tools use this
  field when available.  An authentication provider may already supply it.
* **`section`** identifies the user's class section.  CAT-SOOP's group helpers
  use `section` by default, and courses can also use it for section-specific
  schedules or content.  The field name can be changed with
  `cs_user_config["section_variable"]`.
* **`nsubmits_extra`** grants extra submissions to selected questions.  Its
  value is a list of `(regular_expression, additional_submissions)` pairs.
  The expression is matched against the dot-separated page path and question
  name:

  ```python
  nsubmits_extra = [
      (r"homework\.hw01\..*", 2),
      (r"labs\.lab02\.simulation", 1),
  ]
  ```

* **`full_name`** is a widely used course convention for a roster name.  It is
  available through `cs_user_info`, but CAT-SOOP core normally uses `name`;
  course-specific reports and tools must explicitly choose to read
  `full_name`.

Authentication and saved account information may supplement or override
identity fields such as `name` and `email`.  Use `role`, `section`, and other
course-specific fields in `__USERS__` when they belong to this course rather
than to the user's global account.

<catsoop-section>Course-Specific Fields</catsoop-section>

Any other variables defined by the file are also made available in
`cs_user_info`.  For example:

```python
role = "Student"
full_name = "Ada Lovelace"
section = "L01"
registration_status = "registered"
project_team = "analytical-engine"
```

Page or plugin code can read them normally:

```python
team = cs_user_info.get("project_team")
```

Fields such as `registration_status`, imported roster metadata, or an
`extensions` list have no universal meaning in CAT-SOOP core.  They take
effect only when course code or a plugin reads them.  Document such fields
alongside the custom code that implements their behavior.

<catsoop-section>How User Information Is Loaded</catsoop-section>

The normal sequence is:

1. The configured authentication method identifies the username and may
   provide `name` and `email`.
2. CAT-SOOP executes the matching course file
   `__USERS__/USERNAME.py`, if it exists.
3. If `permissions` is still absent, CAT-SOOP derives it from `role` and
   `cs_permissions`.
4. Saved login and extra-account information is merged into the result.
5. The completed dictionary is exposed to page code as `cs_user_info`.

If no user file exists, the authenticated user can still enter the course.
Their authentication-provided role is used when present; otherwise
`cs_default_role` applies.

User files are executable server-side Python, not data submitted by students.
Keep them under the same source control and review practices as `preload.py`.
Do not put passwords or other reusable credentials in them.

<catsoop-section>Reading Users From Course Code</catsoop-section>

For the current visitor, use `cs_user_info`:

```python
username = cs_user_info["username"]
role = cs_user_info.get("role")
section = cs_user_info.get("section", "default")
```

Code that needs another course user's file can use the user helper:

```python
uinfo = csm_user.read_user_file(
    globals(), cs_course, "ada", default={}
)
```

`read_user_file` returns the variables from the file and adds `username`.
Treat all metadata as possibly absent unless the course enforces it when
creating roster files.
